- Detected divergent routing behavior between 44.192.100.100 (Celer Bridge) and 44.192.0.100 despite both being in the same RPKI-valid /16, and correlated it with unauthenticated AltDB IRR objects (route 44.192.100.0/24 origin AS-AWS, and AS-LEGITAS-SET fraudulently claiming AS-AWS membership, both submitted 2022-08-17 by an apparent single malicious submitter).
- Initial hypothesis: active sub-prefix hijack. Installed defensive blackhole for 44.192.100.0/24 to fail-fast rather than expose DeFi users to potential redirection.
- Coordinated investigation with TransitAS (who reproduced the divergence) and their Tier1 upstream. Root cause turned out to be an AWS-side missing internal route for their own /16 producing an ICMP-redirect loop, not a hijack in the transit chain.
- AWS installed a covering blackhole for 44.192.0.0/16 with 44.192.100.100/32 as an active more-specific, fixing the observable loop; independent tests from my loopback and from TransitAS's loopback confirmed the legitimate RPKI-valid path.
- Lifted my defensive blackhole once AWS remediation was independently verified.
- AWS is pursuing AltDB takedown of the fraudulent objects using their ARIN allocation + RPKI ROA as proof of holdership; TransitAS's upstream reaffirmed strict RPKI-ROV enforcement; I confirmed the same posture on my side (prefer RPKI-valid, reject RPKI-invalid).

Key takeaway: The RPKI ROA plus comparative traceroute (comparing paths to two addresses within the same covering prefix) was the decisive diagnostic — it exposed a routing anomaly that would have been invisible from any single ping, and the anomaly warranted defensive action even before the root cause was fully understood, since the cost of a false-positive blackhole (temporary hard fail) was far below the cost of a false-negative (silent DeFi redirection).
